<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pelagius of Asturias</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/737">737</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Pelayo, Don Pelayo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pelagius was a Visigothic nobleman who founded the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, ruling from about 718 until 737. He led a revolt against Muslim rule in the mountains of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and defeated a Muslim force at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-covadonga">Battle of Covadonga</a>, traditionally dated 722, which later tradition saw as the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a>. His daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ermesinda">Ermesinda</a> married the future Alfonso I.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pelagius-of-asturias.jpg" alt="Pelagius of Asturias" width="320" height="476" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pelayo_en_la_batalla_de_Covadonga_BNE_Mss_2805_f_23r.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pelagius was born around 685, but the details of his early life are largely unknown. He was traditionally depicted with Visigothic origin, though recent scholarship increasingly supports the idea that he was a nobleman of Hispano-Roman descent, linked to the local Asturian elite. He is believed to be the son of Favila. The Chronica Albeldense, written three centuries after his death, says Favila was a dux of Gallaecia killed by the Visigothic king Wittiza, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-alfonso-iii">Chronicle of Alfonso III</a> calls Pelagius a grandson of Chindasuinth and says his father was blinded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> at Wittiza's instigation. Both agree that Wittiza exiled him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>.</p><p>A later tradition tells of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munuza">Munuza</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> governor of Iegione, who was attracted to Pelagius's sister and sent word to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tariq-ibn-ziyad">Tariq ibn Ziyad</a>, who ordered Pelagius captured and sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. If Munuza's seat was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gijon">Gijón</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> had already established their rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, and Pelagius may not have led a local resistance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>At some point Pelagius is said to have rebelled, for reasons that are unknown; such rebellions by local authorities were a common theme in Visigothic Spain. A Muslim army under the command of Alkama and the Christian bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, Oppa, was sent against him. In a battle near Covadonga Alkama was killed and Oppa captured. Moorish chronicles describe Pelagius and his small force as thirty wild donkeys. Dates for the battle vary between sources.</p><p>Pelagius was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeps">princeps</a> of the Asturians by the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> in the Visigothic manner and made his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cangas-de-onis">Cangas de Onís</a>. His kingdom, at first centred on the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, soon grew. He married his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ermesinda">Ermesinda</a> to the future king Alfonso I, son of his eastern neighbour, Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-of-cantabria">Peter of Cantabria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pelagius reigned for eighteen or nineteen years until his death in 737 and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/favila-of-asturias">Fafila</a>. He was buried in the church of Santa Eulalia de Abamia, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cangas-de-onis">Cangas de Onís</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-x-of-castile">Alfonso X of Castile</a> later moved his remains, with those of his wife Gaudiosa and his sister, to the Holy Cave of Covadonga.</p><p>He is credited with initiating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a>, the Christian reconquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, and with establishing the Asturian monarchy. The chief sources for his life are two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> chronicles written in the late ninth century in the kingdom he founded, the Chronica Albeldensia of about 881 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-alfonso-iii">Chronicle of Alfonso III</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pelagius of Asturias” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Pelagius</b>’s son and successor Favila was killed by a bear in 739, after only two years as king, according to the Asturian chronicles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Alfonso married the daughter of <b>Pelagius</b> and became Alfonso I of Asturias in 739, uniting the duchy with the Kingdom of Asturias.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-cantabria">Duchy of Cantabria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Cangas de Onís, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.35,-5.13&amp;year=727" aria-label="Cangas de Onís on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Pelagius of Asturias"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4682 3862h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4682 3862h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=43.35,-5.13&amp;year=727">What was here in 727 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
